frame(1,'Sōðlice, mid-ðȳ-ðe ðæs cyninges dohtor geseah ðæt Apollonius on eallum gōdum cræftum swā wel wæs getogen, ðā gefēoll hiere mōd on his lufe.','The daughter sees an asserted fact, hence indicative wæs in the content clause. Wæs getogen describes his education/training in good accomplishments, not physical pulling. Gefēoll has nominative mōd as its singular subject; hiere is the possessor and on his lufe gives the figurative falling-in-love result. Supply the complete idiom and the rare participle.',['U18G-FB175','U18G-FB60','U18G-FB104','U18G-FB295'])
frame(2,'ðū līefdest mē, lȳtle ǣr, ðæt ic mōste giefan Apollonio swā-hwæt-swā ic wolde of ðīnum goldhorde.','Līefdest is permission, not belief; mē is dative person permitted. The source itself says translate ðæt ic mōste by English to: past subjunctive mōste plus plain giefan gives the permitted action. The free-choice object swā-hwæt-swā has a first-singular past subjunctive wolde; its understood action is giving. Of + dative identifies the treasure from which the gift is drawn. The complete permission-and-selection construction is supplied.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB303','U18G-FB104','U18G-FB105'])
frame(3,'Gief him swā-hwæt-swā ðū wille.','Singular command to the daughter: give him whatever you wish. Him is recipient. Wille is second-singular present subjunctive inside the free-choice relative, under the source note to197; it is not first-person indicative despite identical surface.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB105'])
frame(4,'Hēo ðā swīðe blīðe ūtēode,','The daughter went out very gladly. The exact prefixed finite form ūtēode is supplied; prior supplied occurrences do not make a complete ūt-gān paradigm productive.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB80'])
frame(5,'ic giefe ðē, be mīnes fæder lēafe, twā hund punda goldes, and fēower hund punda gewihte seolfres, and ðone mǣstan dǣl dēorwurðes rēafes, and twēntig ðēowa manna.','First-singular indicative giefe presents the gifts; ðē is dative recipient. Be mīnes fæder lēafe means with my father’s permission, and all following quantities share the gift-object role. The source/nominal packet supplies complete quantity and weight analyses. Preserve ðēowa without r. A strong-noun genitive plural with appositional manna is a bounded editorial inference from the attested spelling and phrase; supply the whole phrase case and meaning without testing that historical lexical identity. No general production of these numeral constructions is inferred.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB176-DETAIL'])
frame(6,'Berað ðās ðing mid ēow, ðe ic behēt Apollonio mīnum lārēowe, and lęcgeað innan būre beforan mīnum frēondum.','Two plural commands address the slaves: carry the gifts with you and place them in the chamber before my friends. The promise relative has first-singular past behēt, accusative gifts represented by ðe, and dative Apollonio mīnum lārēowe. The exact source imperative lęcgeað is supplied separately from the newly displayed indicative plural of identical spelling. Source būr has local dining-chamber sense, explained in the original note.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB176-DETAIL','U18G-FB94'])
frame(7,'Ðis wearð ðā ðus gedōn, æfter ðǣre cwēne hǣse;','Singular passive event: this was done thus according to the princess’s command. Gedōn is the supplied past participle do/perform; it is not the newly taught present subjunctive causative gedōn even though the spelling matches that plural.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB101'])
frame(8,'and ealle ðā męnn hiere giefa hęredon ðe hīe gesāwon.','All the men who saw the gifts praised them. The relative ðe resumes męnn, expressly supplied by original note13; hīe is accusative plural referring to gifts. Both verbs are past indicative third plural. Keep the source note visible; do not assess the already supplied antecedent as independent discovery.',['U18G-FB176'])
frame(9,'Ðā sōðlice geęndode se gebēorscipe, and ðā męnn ealle ārison, and grētton ðone cyning and ðā cwēne, and bǣdon hīe gesunde bēon, and hām gewęndon.','Geęndode is intransitive: the banquet ended. The men then rose, took leave of king and princess, wished them well, and returned home. Grētan’s departure sense is previously productive. Bǣdon hīe gesunde bēon is the supplied farewell wish: hīe is accusative subject of infinitive bēon, and gesunde its agreeing plural predicate. The finite bǣdon governs the complete wished-for state; the familiar request sense alone did not establish this special farewell construction.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-EX64','U18G-FB96','U18G-FB106'])
frame(10,'Ðū gōda cyning, and earmra gemiltsiend, and ðū cwēn, lāre lufiend, bēon gē gesunde.','Two addressed people are resumed by plural gē; bēon is second-plural present subjunctive expressing farewell well-wishing, not an infinitive or a third-plural declaration. The agent nouns and their genitive complements are supplied in the nominal packet. The original note translates the wish by Latin valete.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB103'])
frame(11,'Hē beseah ēac tō ðǣm ðēowum mannum, ðe ðæt mǣden him forgiefen hæfde, and him cwæð tō:','He looked toward the slaves whom the maiden had given him, and spoke to them. Beseah with tō/dative is supplied. Forgiefen hæfde is past-perfect active giving: maiden is subject, slaves relative object, and him the Apollonius recipient. The final him instead resumes the slaves, and postposed tō belongs to the speech-to-them frame. Preserve the actual prior bare-participle analysis of forgiefen; add current context separately.',['U18G-FB176','U18G-FB102','U18G-FB106'])
frame(12,'Nimað ðās ðing mid ēow, ðe mē sēo cwēn forgeaf, and gān wē sēcean ūre giesthūs, ðæt wē mægen ūs geręstan.','Plural command take these things with you, then first-plural subjunctive exhortation gān wē, let us go. Sēcean is the exact supplied infinitive spelling of seek and expresses the purpose of going. The gift relative has princess as singular subject and mē as recipient. The final purpose has first-plural subjunctive mægen and reflexive rest: ūs is accusative, as original note177.2 expressly directs to184b. Geręstan is a supplied infinitive, not granted by the earlier finite forms.',['U18G-FB177','U18G-FB80','U18G-FB101','U18G-FB103','U18G-FB106'])
spec='geseah:it3s wæs:it3s getogen:pp gefēoll:it3s cwæð:it3s līefdest:it2s mōste:st1s giefan:inf wolde:st1s cwæð:it3s gief:m2s wille:sp2s ūtēode:it3s cwæð:it3s giefe:ip1s cwæð:it3s berað:m2p behēt:it1s lęcgeað:m2p wearð:it3s gedōn:pp hęredon:it3p gesāwon:it3p geęndode:it3s ārison:it3p grētton:it3p bǣdon:it3p bēon:inf gewęndon:it3p cwæð:it3s bēon:sp2p beseah:it3s forgiefen:pp hæfde:it3s cwæð:it3s nimað:m2p forgeaf:it3s gān:sp1p sēcean:inf mægen:sp1p geręstan:inf'
special={'getogen':('geteon','getēon','train; educate'),'gefēoll':('gefeallan','gefeallan','fall, figuratively of the heart falling in love'),'līefdest':('liefan-permit','līefan','allow; permit'),'ūtēode':('utgan','ūtgān','go out'),'behēt':('behatan','behātan','promise'),'lęcgeað':('lecgean','lęcgean','lay; place'),'gedōn':('gedon','gedōn','do; perform'),'geęndode':('geendian','geęndian','end; come to an end'),'beseah':('beseon','besēon','look toward'),'sēcean':('secan','sēcan','seek'),'geręstan':('gerestan','geręstan','rest; repose')}
